  "contract": "Synchronous update validation (published Server 2.11.1, ValidatedExternalWorkflowUpdateAdmission and WorkflowUpdateValidationTaskBroker). Server capabilities advertise update_validation_tasks and the synchronous_update_validation contract with a 10-second caller wait and a 5-second claim. A raw worker declares update_validators for two of three updates, so the run's WorkflowStarted records declared_update_validators. Before validation, an update without request_id and one with invalid arguments are refused and record nothing. A validated update waits while a worker claims its validation task through the multiplexed workflow poll (task_kinds) or the dedicated poll; the task carries the run's replay history and history export, update arguments, attempt and lease. Approval admits the update with update_id equal to the task id and UpdateAccepted with request_id; a validator rejection records a rejected command and update under the task id with UpdateRejected and its validation errors, and answers 422; any other worker failure answers 409 and records nothing. Completions are fenced by lease owner, attempt, lease expiry and worker freshness, and a terminal task refuses duplicates. Retries with the same request_id return the recorded receipt or the stored failure; changed arguments conflict. Nobody claiming, a lost worker and a worker that never answers each time out with 504 after the wait. An expired lease is claimed again with the next attempt. The multiplexed poll alternates kinds after each lease. A worker contract without update_validators refuses every remote update; no capable or matching worker, and no worker at all, refuse with 409. Identifiers, timestamps, the requester and the control-plane block are normalized or left to their own fixtures."
}
